WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.870
Marie Curie
Marie Curie’s discoveries in radiation changed

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:03.870
the world.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:08.000
She became one of the most important women
in science and her research is still important

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.580
to scientists and doctors today.She became
the first person - male or female - to win

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:14.290
the Nobel Prize twice.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:19.320
And Marie’s discovery of the element radium
helped unlock the mysteries of the atom.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.730
Yet she came from the most unlikely of circumstances.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:27.619
Marie Curie showed that through hard work
and determination anything is possible.

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:32.029
In this week’s Biographics, we meet Marie
Curie, scientific pioneer.

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:35.510
It was like a new world opened to me, the
world of science

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:48.429
Beginnings
Maria Salomee Sklodowska was born on November

00:00:48.429 --> 00:00:51.589
7th, 1867 in Warsaw, Poland.

00:00:51.589 --> 00:00:56.750
She was the fifth child born to Vladislav
and Bronislawa Sklodowska, who were both teachers.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:01.539
Vladislav taught high school physics and mathematics,
and although Bronislawa gave birth to five

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:05.939
children in eight years and had tuberculosis,
she was the full-time director of a private

00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:07.149
school for girls.

00:01:07.149 --> 00:01:11.080
It was only after the birth of Maria that
she retired.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:15.979
Through a series of bad investments the family
had lost their savings, requiring them to

00:01:15.979 --> 00:01:19.500
board at the private school where Bronislawa
had taught.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.080
This meant that, for the children, there was
no privacy and they had to keep quiet and

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.670
be on their best behavior at all times.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.210
Bronislawa suffered from Tuberculosis.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:32.799
Terrified of passing the lung disease on to
her children, she refused to hug them or show

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:34.780
any physical affection.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.659
When Maria, who was known as Manya, was around
five, the family managed to get a place of

00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:42.799
their own, but they had to take in boarders
to make ends meet.

00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:47.710
Manya didn’t have her own bedroom but had
to sleep on the sofa in the dining room.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:50.320
She had to get up extra early to set the table
for breakfast.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.770
Manya, was fascinated with the instruments
that her father, who was a teacher of physics,

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:57.930
kept in his little study - especially the
electroscope.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:02.920
When she started school in 1874, aged six,
she was the youngest and the smallest child

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:03.920
there.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.369
However, she soon proved herself to be among
the smartest students in her class.

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:13.180
She had an excellent memory so she was often
selected to recite poetry for visitors.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:17.879
This was something that the intensely shy
girl detested.

00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:21.180
Life in Poland was tough during the latter
part of the 19th century.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.030
Warsaw was under Russian rule.

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:27.569
Laws forbade the speaking of Polish or the
learning of polish history.

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:32.790
However, these laws backfired, causing many
Poles, including Manya’s family, to be even

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.909
more proud of their Polish heritage.

00:02:34.909 --> 00:02:39.010
Manya dreamed of doing great things and bringing
honor to their country.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:43.780
When Manya was eight, her older sister Zofia,
caught typhus fever and died.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.340
About two years later, Manya’s mother died
from tuberculosis.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:51.629
The little girl was inconsolable - the two
dearest people in her life - her sister and

00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:54.799
her mother - had been cruelly snatched away
from her.

00:02:54.799 --> 00:02:58.390
Despite her despair, Manya was able to maintain
top grades at school.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:02.450
With the love and support of her father and
remaining siblings, she began to dream about

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:03.930
an accomplished future.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:06.989
In 1883 she completed her high school education.

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:10.540
She was first in her class in every subject.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:13.770
Shortly thereafter, however, she became ill
with what she called ‘ fatigue of growth

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:14.849
and study.’

00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:18.180
She spent a year recovering with relatives
in the country.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:24.500
Out in the World
On her return, Marya was intent on furthering

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.080
her education in the sciences.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.849
But the Russian government forbade any Polish
women from attending the University of Warsaw.

00:03:30.849 --> 00:03:33.209
Maria made a pact with her older sister Bronya.

00:03:33.209 --> 00:03:38.030
They would pool their finances so that first,
Bronya, and then Marya, could attend the University

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:39.030
of Paris.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.790
Marya spent the next six years as a governess,
which meant that she was little more than

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:44.590
a servant to a wealthy family.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:48.240
For three and a half of those years, she lived
with the Zorawski family, sixty miles from

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:49.240
Warsaw.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.562
She tutored two of the Zorawski children seven
hours a day and was at the family’s beck

00:03:51.562 --> 00:03:54.580
and call the remainder of the time.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:58.150
With the father’s approval, she also taught
local peasant children to read and write,

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:01.689
an illegal activity for which she could have
been severely punished.

00:04:01.689 --> 00:04:05.109
She used every moment of her precious free
time for study.

00:04:05.109 --> 00:04:09.559
During this time, Marya also began attending
secret meetings of the ‘Floating University’.

00:04:09.559 --> 00:04:13.079
Members read about scientific and other works
that were banned by the Russians because they

00:04:13.079 --> 00:04:15.430
thought that they might stir up rebellious
ideas.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:20.630
After a year of living with the Zorawski family,
Maria met their oldest son, who was home from

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:21.630
University.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:24.090
He and Marya, now aged 19, quickly fell in
love.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.570
However, the Zorawski’s refused to let their
son marry a poor governess.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:31.790
The unhappy couple separated, but Marya kept
working for the family because Bronya was

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.090
depending on her to help pay her way.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:36.930
To get over heartbreak, Marya threw herself
into her studies.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:38.830
She focused on physics and chemistry.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:43.720
In 1889, her employment with the Zorawski’s
ended and she moved back to Warsaw where she

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.530
began working for another family.

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:49.981
One of her cousin’s ran Warsaw’s Museum
of Industry and Agriculture, which was a lab

00:04:49.981 --> 00:04:58.320
where Polish students, including Marya, could
study science.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:04:59.320
The Sorbonne
By 1891, Marya had managed to save enough

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:01.310
money for her to join Bronya in Paris.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:05.890
It was here that she changed to mMarie, the
French version of her name.

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:09.660
Living with Bronya and her new husband, Marie
attended the Sorbonne University.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:13.630
Before long she tired of the commute and moved
in to the student area, living in a tiny attic

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:16.560
with no proper lighting and only a coal stove.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.570
She had very little money, ate poorly, had
to pay for her lessons, and worked long hours

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:22.440
in the University library.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:27.190
Yet she found her work so satisfying that
the hardships were worth it.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:31.100
Marie had to work much harder than her classmates
due to her limited understanding of the French

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.100
language.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:35.760
Still, she graduated in 1893, having earned
a master’s degree in physics.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:37.990
She also got the top marks in her class.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:41.930
In addition, she won a scholarship, which
allowed her to begin studying for a second

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:45.010
degree, this time in mathematics.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:49.900
Pierre
While studying for her mathematics degree,

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:54.430
Marie got a job studying the magnetism of
various types of steel for a French company.

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:56.960
To do the work, however, she needed to find
a lab.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.680
A friend introduced her to a man who could
help her to do so.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.240
His name was Pierre Curie, a teacher and head
of the lab at the School of Industrial Physics

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:05.580
and Chemistry in Paris.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.880
He was already famous for his work with crystals
and magnets.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.000
Marie began doing her investigations on the
different types of steel as Pierre continued

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:15.920
researching the effect of electricity on crystals.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:19.960
No one was more surprised than Curie himself,
when he became attracted to Marie.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.070
This man of science considered most women
to be a waste of space.

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:24.880
Yet Marie was different.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:27.950
She was brilliant and she had a deep love
for science.

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:31.410
Instead of wooing her with flowers, Pierre
attempted to steal Marie’s heart by giving

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:34.870
her an autographed copy of one of his physics
papers.

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:37.370
Pierre asked Marie to marry him shortly after
they met.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:41.480
Marie wanted to say yes, but felt that if
she did marry Pierre, she would never again

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:43.590
live in her beloved Poland.

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:47.990
After completing her mathematics degree, she
returned to Warsaw for a holiday, unsure if

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:49.630
she would ever go back to Paris.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:53.020
However, she was bombarded with love letters
by Pierre.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:58.320
She finally decided to return in order to
continue her education and to see Pierre.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.810
Marie and Pierre were married on July 26th,
1895.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:06.110
The ever practical Marie wore a dark blue
suit that she could later wear in the lab.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:09.960
For the next year, she worked in Pierre’s
lab studying steel and magnetism.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:14.180
In August, 1896 she gave birth to Irene, their
first child.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:16.490
Becoming a mother, though, didn’t slow her
down.

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:20.500
She decided to earn a doctorate in physics
and needed a new topic to study.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:24.750
She had heard about a French physicist named
Henri Becquerel and his discovery of mysterious

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:27.020
uranium rays in 1896.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:33.170
The subject fascinated her and she decided
to make it the focus of her doctorate.

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:37.840
Interest in X-Rays
In November, 1895, Wilhelm Roentgen, a German

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.610
physicist, discovered invisible penetrating
rays coming from an electric tube in one of

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:43.610
his experiments.

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:46.030
He called them x-rays, because he did not
know what they were.

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:50.190
They had the ability to pass through flesh
and other substances, but not through hard,

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:53.590
dense materials like bone and thick metals.

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:56.330
X-rays and their effects became world famous
within months.

00:07:56.330 --> 00:08:01.420
In 1896, the french physicist Henri Becquerel
discovered more kinds of penetrating rays.

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:05.930
Unlike Roentgen's, which were made by an electrical
effect, these rays seemed to come naturally

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:07.610
from a piece of uranium.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:11.660
Becquerel had left the uranium lying on a
sealed packet of photographic paper for several

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:14.450
days in a drawer, and it caused the paper
to mist over.

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:19.030
The uranium seemed to produce some kind of
invisible rays that could pass through the

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:20.720
thick black paper.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:23.160
Most scientists ignored Becquerel’s uranium
rays.

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:24.710
But not Marie Curie.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:29.740
She threw herself into the study, first systematically
searching for uranium-type rays coming from

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:30.900
other elements.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:34.709
Checking through all 70 elements she found
that thorium also gave off rays.

00:08:34.709 --> 00:08:37.550
Next, Marie analyzed rocks that contained
more than one element.

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:43.439
As she expected, most of the uranium-type
rays were given off by the rocks called pitchblende.

00:08:43.439 --> 00:08:46.050
This was because they contained uranium or
thorium.

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:52.470
But to her surprise, pitchblende gave off
more radiation than she expected.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:56.060
What was causing the extra radiation?

00:08:56.060 --> 00:09:00.670
The Great Discovery
After checking her results more than twenty

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:05.459
times, Maria announced, in July, 1898, that
she had found a new element.

00:09:05.459 --> 00:09:07.880
She named it Polonium after her beloved Poland.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:13.410
At the same time she invented the word ‘radioactive’
to describe polonium, uranium and thorium.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:19.220
Then, in December 1898, Marie announced the
discovery of another new, even more radioactive,

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:21.100
element - radium.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.769
But years of work lay ahead.

00:09:23.769 --> 00:09:27.990
Marie and Pierre had to prove to fellow scientists
that polonium and radium existed.

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:32.459
To do this, they had to produce the two elements
in their purest forms, find the weight of

00:09:32.459 --> 00:09:37.430
the atoms, and show that each element has
an atomic weight different from any other

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:38.430
element’s.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:42.339
But their first focus was to produce pure
radium.

00:09:42.339 --> 00:09:46.620
To do this they needed two things - lots of
pitchblende and a large lab.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:50.870
The pitchblende came from a mine in Austria
and the Curie’s were able to buy it up very

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:51.870
cheaply.

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:53.910
Finding lab space was harder.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:57.649
The only room available was a cold, leaky
shed near Pierre’s work, but it gave them

00:09:57.649 --> 00:09:59.220
the sauce they needed.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:03.750
Marie had to melt pitchblende in huge pots
and stir it with a steel rod almost as tall

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:04.810
as he was.

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:09.540
Despite her hard work, she was determined
to discover all she could about radioactivity.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:13.610
Between 1900 and 1903, Marie published many
papers on her work.

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:17.079
During this period she was also completing
her doctoral degree and trying to produce

00:10:17.079 --> 00:10:18.140
pure radium.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:21.370
As if that wasn’t enough, in October, 1900,
she began teaching at a teacher’s college

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.370
in Sevres, a Parisian suburb.

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:26.550
And, of course, she was raising her daughter
Irene.

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:32.460
On July 21st, 1902, Marie finally reported
the weight of one radium atom - this was a

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:34.040
major breakthrough.

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:36.990
Because the weight of the radium atom was
different from the atomic weight of any other

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:41.680
element, it proved that Marie had discovered
a new element.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:46.029
Marie and Pierre could have become rich by
claiming all rights to working with radium.

00:10:46.029 --> 00:10:50.740
Instead, they shared their information, telling
how they’d purified the element, and more.

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:54.360
They believed scientific research should benefit
everyone.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:11:00.500
Accolades
In June, 1903, Marie became the first woman

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:02.820
in Europe to receive a doctorate in science.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:07.769
But, as the fame of the uber science couple
grew, their physical health began to deteriorate.

00:11:07.769 --> 00:11:10.180
Pierre was constantly tired and in pain.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:12.889
Marie was also in a constant state of exhaustion.

00:11:12.889 --> 00:11:16.560
She had recently had a miscarriage and had
lost a lot of weight.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:19.680
They both suffered from burnt, numb fingertips.

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:23.240
We now know that they were suffering from
the effects of radiation exposure.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.620
At the time, radium was being touted for its
health benefits.

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:31.190
Because it could kill healthy tissue, doctors
tested it on diseased cells and found it also

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:32.499
destroyed them.

00:11:32.499 --> 00:11:36.639
Soom radium was being used to stop cancer
cells from growing in patients with cancer.

00:11:36.639 --> 00:11:39.019
The new treatment was called ‘Curietherapy.’

00:11:39.019 --> 00:11:44.139
In November , 1903 Marie and Pierre were given
the Humphry Davy Medal, England’s highest

00:11:44.139 --> 00:11:45.540
award in chemistry.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:50.060
But the greatest accolade came a month later,
when they, along with Henri Becquerel, won

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:51.779
the Nobel Prize for Physics.

00:11:51.779 --> 00:11:54.970
The Nobel Prize came with a substantial cash
price.

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:58.170
This allowed the Curie’s, for the first
time, to hire a lab assistant.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:02.139
Now reporters and visitors wanted to meet
the famous scientific couple.

00:12:02.139 --> 00:12:06.490
Some eager reporters even attempted to interview
the Curie’s young daughter, Irene.

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:10.300
Nobel Prize winners had to travel to Sweden
to receive their award and speak about their

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:11.300
work.

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:15.329
But Marie was too ill to go.It wasn’t until
1905 that she was able to make the trip.

00:12:15.329 --> 00:12:18.050
The couple’s feelings about the prize were
mixed.

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:22.320
Marie was proud of her work and her accomplishment
of being the first woman to achieve world

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:23.320
fame as a scientist.

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:27.319
The prize money also helped to fund their
research, which they had so far been paying

00:12:27.319 --> 00:12:28.639
out of their own pocket.

00:12:28.639 --> 00:12:33.749
But they disliked the fame, which interfered
with their work.

00:12:33.749 --> 00:12:37.110
In 1904, Marie gave birth to a second daughter,
Eve.

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:39.680
Shortly thereafter Pierre was made a professor
at the Sorbonne.

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:43.579
He was given a better laboratory and Marie
was paid as his chief assistant.

00:12:43.579 --> 00:12:46.850
The next year he was elected to the Academy
of Sciences.

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:53.189
Tragedy
For two years, things went well for the Curies.

00:12:53.189 --> 00:12:57.300
They were finally comfortable financially
and were respected leaders in their field.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.879
Then in April,1906 tragedy struck.

00:12:59.879 --> 00:13:04.399
They had just enjoyed a relaxed weekend in
the country and we're back in a rainy Paris.

00:13:04.399 --> 00:13:08.139
A rushed off his feet Pierre was running errands
in the rain when he stepped out to cross the

00:13:08.139 --> 00:13:10.689
road and fell in front of a horse drawn wagon.

00:13:10.689 --> 00:13:12.300
The startled horse reared up.

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:15.620
It’s hooves missed Pierre but the rear wheels
crushed his skull.

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:16.980
He was killed instantly.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:20.740
When word spread that a famous scientist had
been killed, the crowd had to be stopped from

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:22.759
attacking the innocent driver.

00:13:22.759 --> 00:13:26.240
Marie didn’t hear about the tragedy until
she returned home that night.

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:28.899
She was devastated by her husband's death.

00:13:28.899 --> 00:13:31.529
However, she remembered what he had once told
her . . .

00:13:31.529 --> 00:13:37.449
Whatever happens, even if one should become
like a body without a soul, still one must

00:13:37.449 --> 00:13:38.449
work.

00:13:38.449 --> 00:13:41.209
So, when she was offered her husband’s job
at the Sorbonne, she accepted.

00:13:41.209 --> 00:13:45.339
Before her first class, reporters, students
and curious people crowded the hall to see

00:13:45.339 --> 00:13:47.499
the first female teacher at the institute.

00:13:47.499 --> 00:13:51.279
They wondered if she would praise her husband
- or break down under the strain.

00:13:51.279 --> 00:13:55.189
Instead, she began quietly speaking - picking
up at the very spot where Pierre had left

00:13:55.189 --> 00:13:57.240
off during his last lecture.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:01.949
One of Marie's deepest regrets was that Pierre
had died without ever having his own permanent

00:14:01.949 --> 00:14:02.949
lab.

00:14:02.949 --> 00:14:07.459
It gave her great satisfaction when, in 1909,
plans were begun to establish the Radium Institute

00:14:07.459 --> 00:14:08.459
in Paris.

00:14:08.459 --> 00:14:14.649
It would have a laboratory that Marie would
supervise, to be called the Curie Pavilion.

00:14:14.649 --> 00:14:20.980
Pure Radium
Working as hard as ever, Marie noted that

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:25.910
Lord Kelvin, a Scottish physicist, had suggested
that radium was not an element, since it had

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:29.420
been found to give off helium gas, which is
itself an element.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:32.949
She continued to make even purer polonium
and radium.

00:14:32.949 --> 00:14:38.029
By 1910 she had produced pure radium, and
showed that it was a brilliant-white metal.

00:14:38.029 --> 00:14:41.860
She even found its melting , 700 degrees celsius.

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:47.019
In 1910, Marie also published her 971-page
work Treatise on Radioactivity.

00:14:47.019 --> 00:14:52.509
In 1911 came another great honor - the Nobel
Prize for Chemistry, awarded to her alone,

00:14:52.509 --> 00:14:54.100
for making pure radium.

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:58.880
In 1911, Radiology Congress, an international
group of radiation scientists, decided they

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:03.350
needed a new unit of measurement for radiation
- they called it the ‘Curie’.

00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:07.250
Marie insisted that she was the only one who
could take on the task of working out the

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:09.370
exact size of a curie unit.

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:13.089
It was painstaking work, but she did it in
Pierre’s honor.

00:15:13.089 --> 00:15:16.759
In 1911, Marie failed to be elected to the
French Academy of Sciences.

00:15:16.759 --> 00:15:20.269
Many people said that it was simply because
she was a woman - her scientific work was

00:15:20.269 --> 00:15:22.019
of the highest quality.

00:15:22.019 --> 00:15:26.709
Her personal life was followed by the newspapers,and
there was great interest in her friendship

00:15:26.709 --> 00:15:30.490
with physicist colleague Paul Langevin, who
had left his wife.

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:32.209
Extremely upset, Marie fell ill.

00:15:32.209 --> 00:15:35.269
After treatment she stayed with her friend
Hertha Ayrton in England.

00:15:35.269 --> 00:15:39.009
By 1914, the Radium Institute at the University
of Paris was finished.

00:15:39.009 --> 00:15:41.329
The same year, World War One began.

00:15:41.329 --> 00:15:42.980
Marie threw herself into the war effort.

00:15:42.980 --> 00:15:46.759
She organized x-ray equipment for hospitals
to help them locate bullets and shrapnel in

00:15:46.759 --> 00:15:48.320
the wounds of injured soldiers.

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:52.019
She also set up a course to train people in
the use of radiography units.

00:15:52.019 --> 00:15:57.059
At war’s end in 1918, Marie became Director
of the Paris Radium Institute.

00:15:57.059 --> 00:16:00.160
The Institute became a world center for radiation
physics and chemistry.

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:04.550
In 1920, Marie became friends with Marie Maloney,
an American journalist.

00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:08.579
Maloney helped to improve Curie’s public
image, and planned to raise money for the

00:16:08.579 --> 00:16:11.189
Radium Institute by a lecture tour of the
USA.

00:16:11.189 --> 00:16:15.129
Although her hearing and sight were failing,
she carried out part of the tour before illness

00:16:15.129 --> 00:16:17.050
forced her to return to France.

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:21.089
Many universities awarded her special degrees,
and the Women of America gave her one gram

00:16:21.089 --> 00:16:26.589
of radium, worth a hundred thousand dollars,
in recognition of her work and achievements

00:16:26.589 --> 00:16:28.200
as a woman scientist.

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:36.490
In 1922, she was at last elected to the French
Academy of Medicine.

00:16:36.490 --> 00:16:39.019
The Decline
In the early 1920’s scientists finally realized

00:16:39.019 --> 00:16:40.779
the dangers of radiation.

00:16:40.779 --> 00:16:43.449
People working with radioactive material began
taking precautions.

00:16:43.449 --> 00:16:45.630
But it was too late for Marie.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:49.450
Her work with radiation probably caused the
cataracts in her eyes that now threatened

00:16:49.450 --> 00:16:50.769
her with blindness.

00:16:50.769 --> 00:16:54.930
She had four operations to remove the cataracts
but wanted no one to know - she didn’t want

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:57.230
people thinking she was old and helpless.

00:16:57.230 --> 00:17:00.290
Marie continued to oversee the work in her
own labs in Paris.

00:17:00.290 --> 00:17:03.910
She also travelled to raise funds for research
by younger scientists.

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:06.970
In 1928, she had a final operation for cataracts.

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:09.070
She was cared for by her daughter Eve.

00:17:09.070 --> 00:17:13.860
After further illness, she died on July 4th,
1934, at Sancellemoz, Switzerland, as a result

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:18.760
of aplastic anemia, a lack of red blood cells,
caused by long exposure to radiation.

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:21.070
Marie’s legacy lived on in her daughter
Irene.

00:17:21.070 --> 00:17:25.970
In 1935, her and her husband, Frederic, received
the Nobel Prize for chemistry.

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:29.990
In 1995, Marie and Pierre were reburied in
the Pantheon in Paris.

00:17:29.990 --> 00:18:06.000
She is the first woman given that honor based
on her own achievements.

